  "worked_solution": "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (9{e}^{-3t}-4{e}^{-t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9(-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t})-4(-{e}^{-t})+C \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-3t}+4{e}^{-t}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =-3{e}^{0}+4{e}^{0}+C \\\\ 1 & =-3+4+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=4{e}^{-t}-3{e}^{-3t}.$",
